    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Stereographic projection: the Riemann sphere\n\n$\\mathbb{C} \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ is bijectively identified with the unit sphere $S^2$ via stereographic projection from the north pole. The point at infinity is the north pole; $0$ is the south pole; the unit circle is the equator.\n\nThis compactifies $\\mathbb{C}$ — every Möbius transformation extends to a smooth map of $S^2$ (rotations of the sphere are exactly the Möbius transformations $T(z) = (az + b)/(-\\bar b z + \\bar a)$ with $|a|^2 + |b|^2 = 1$, the so-called **special unitary group** $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$).\n\nThe Riemann sphere is the right setting for global complex analysis: **rational functions** $p(z)/q(z)$ are continuous maps $S^2 \\to S^2$.\n\n## What's coming\n\nStage 3 sits on top of three facts about $\\mathbb{C}$:\n\n1. $\\mathbb{C}$ is a field where multiplication has a beautiful geometric interpretation.\n2. The \"complex derivative\" $f'(z) = \\lim (f(z + h) - f(z))/h$ requires the limit to exist as $h \\to 0$ from *all* directions in $\\mathbb{C}$ — much stronger than mere $\\mathbb{R}^2$-differentiability.\n3. Holomorphic functions (those satisfying point 2) are stunningly rigid: analytic ($C^\\infty$ + Taylor series convergent), values determined by behaviour on small sets, etc.\n\nThe next notebook makes precise what \"complex differentiable\" means.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}